from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"In a Linux path, the very top of the filesystem is written as...","options":["C:","/","~","//root"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Linux has one unified tree rooted at / - see ''What Linux is and how the filesystem is laid out''."},{"prompt":"Which command lists the files in the current directory?","options":["ls","cd","list","dir /w"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ls lists directory contents; cd changes directory - see ''Navigating and managing files''."},{"prompt":"The symbol ~ in a path refers to...","options":["the root directory","your home directory","the current directory","the parent directory"],"answer":1,"explanation":"~ expands to your home directory, e.g. /home/noelia."},{"prompt":"To send a command''s output into a file, overwriting it, you use...","options":[">","<","|","&"],"answer":0,"explanation":"> redirects standard output to a file; >> appends instead - see ''Viewing and editing text''."},{"prompt":"The character that joins two commands so the first feeds the second is...","options":["the semicolon ;","the pipe |","the ampersand &","the caret ^"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A pipe | sends one command''s output to the next command''s input - see ''Pipes and filters''."},{"prompt":"Which command changes a file''s permission bits?","options":["chown","chmod","chgrp","chdir"],"answer":1,"explanation":"chmod changes the mode (permissions); chown changes the owner - see ''Permissions and ownership''."},{"prompt":"To see which processes are using the most memory and refresh live, you run...","options":["ps once","top","ls -l","kill"],"answer":1,"explanation":"top shows a live, refreshing table of running processes - see ''Processes and system monitoring''."},{"prompt":"On Debian and Ubuntu, the usual tool to install a package is...","options":["apt","yum only","make","grep"],"answer":0,"explanation":"apt (or apt-get) manages packages on Debian-family systems - see ''Package management''."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'What Linux is and how the filesystem is laid out', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain what the shell is, open a terminal, and read an absolute path against the standard Linux directory tree. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, what single character marks the top of the whole filesystem?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Linux is an operating system kernel; a distribution (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora) bundles it with tools. The shell is a program that reads the commands you type and asks the system to run them; bash is the most common one. You talk to the shell in a terminal. Everything on a Linux system lives in one tree that starts at / (the root). Common branches: /home holds each user''s personal files (your home is /home/yourname, abbreviated ~); /etc holds system configuration; /bin and /usr/bin hold programs; /var holds changing data like logs; /tmp holds temporary files. An absolute path starts at / and names every step (/home/noelia/notes.txt); a relative path starts from where you currently are. The special names . mean ''here'' and .. mean ''one level up''."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Print your current location: pwd might show /home/noelia. See who you are: whoami prints noelia. Read the path /etc/ssh/sshd_config left to right: start at root /, into etc, into ssh, at the file sshd_config. The path ~/projects means /home/noelia/projects. From /home/noelia/projects, the relative path ../music refers to /home/noelia/music, because .. climbs up one level to /home/noelia first. Ask what a command is with type ls, which reports that ls is a program on disk."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What does pwd print? 2) Rewrite ~/photos as an absolute path for user noelia. 3) In a path, what does .. mean? 4) Is /etc an absolute or relative path? 5) Which directory holds system configuration files? Answers: 1) Your current working directory, e.g. /home/noelia. 2) /home/noelia/photos. 3) The parent directory, one level up. 4) Absolute - it begins at the root /. 5) /etc."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming filenames ignore case - Linux treats Report and report as different. Mistake: confusing / (the root of the tree) with ~ (your home directory). Exit: (a) What program reads and runs the commands you type? (b) What does ~ expand to? Answers: the shell (for example bash); your home directory, such as /home/noelia."}]}'::jsonb, 'Linux paths use forward slashes and are case-sensitive: Notes.txt and notes.txt are two different files.', 12),
  (3, 'Navigating and managing files: ls, cd, cp, mv, rm, mkdir', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Move around the filesystem and create, copy, rename, and delete files and directories with confidence. Warm-up: which command shows what is in the directory you are standing in?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"cd DIR moves you into a directory; cd with no argument returns home; cd .. steps up one level. ls lists a directory''s contents, and ls -l shows a long listing with permissions, owner, size, and date, while ls -a also reveals hidden files whose names begin with a dot. mkdir makes a new directory, and mkdir -p makes a whole path of nested directories at once. cp SOURCE DEST copies a file; cp -r copies a directory and everything inside it. mv SOURCE DEST both moves and renames, depending on whether DEST is a directory or a new name. rm deletes a file, and rm -r deletes a directory tree - permanently, with no undo."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Make and enter a folder: mkdir reports then cd reports. Create nested folders in one shot: mkdir -p project/src/utils. List details including hidden files: ls -la. Copy a file: cp notes.txt notes_backup.txt. Copy a whole folder: cp -r project project_copy. Rename a file by moving it to a new name: mv notes.txt README.txt. Move a file into a folder: mv README.txt project/. Delete one file: rm notes_backup.txt. Delete a folder and its contents: rm -r project_copy. Go back up: cd .. returns you to the parent directory."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which command creates the folder path a/b/c in one step? 2) How do you copy the directory site to site_old? 3) What does mv old.txt new.txt do when new.txt does not exist? 4) Which ls flag shows hidden dotfiles? 5) How do you return to your home directory in one command? Answers: 1) mkdir -p a/b/c. 2) cp -r site site_old. 3) It renames old.txt to new.txt. 4) -a (for example ls -a). 5) cd with no argument (or cd ~)."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: running rm -r on the wrong path - there is no trash can, so deletion is final. Mistake: forgetting -r when copying a directory, which makes cp refuse the job. Exit: (a) Which command renames a file? (b) What does the -p flag add to mkdir? Answers: mv (moving a file to a new name renames it); it creates any missing parent directories along the path."}]}'::jsonb, 'rm deletes immediately with no recycle bin. Double-check the target before pressing Enter, especially with rm -r.', 13),
  (4, 'Viewing and editing text: cat, less, nano, and redirection', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Read files at the terminal, make quick edits, and capture command output into files with redirection. Warm-up: what is the difference between > and >>?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"cat prints a whole file to the screen and is handy for short files. less opens a long file in a scrollable pager - press Space to page down, / to search, and q to quit. head shows the first lines of a file and tail the last; tail -f follows a file as it grows, which is perfect for logs. nano is a beginner-friendly editor: you type directly, and the bottom bar lists commands where ^ means the Control key, so ^O saves (writes out) and ^X exits. Redirection reshapes where input and output flow: command > file sends output into file, replacing it; command >> file appends; and command < file feeds a file in as input."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Show a short file: cat notes.txt. Page through a long one: less /var/log/syslog then press q to leave. Peek at the top and bottom: head -n 5 data.csv and tail -n 5 data.csv. Watch a log update live: tail -f app.log. Edit a file: nano todo.txt, type a line, then press Ctrl+O and Enter to save and Ctrl+X to quit. Create a file from a command: echo ''hello world'' > greeting.txt writes one line. Add another line without erasing: echo ''second line'' >> greeting.txt. Confirm the result: cat greeting.txt now shows both lines."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which command scrolls a large file one page at a time? 2) What does echo hi > file.txt do if file.txt already has text? 3) How do you append a line instead? 4) In nano, which key combination saves the file? 5) Which command follows a growing log file? Answers: 1) less. 2) It overwrites the file, erasing the old contents and leaving only ''hi''. 3) Use >> , for example echo hi >> file.txt. 4) Ctrl+O (written ^O), then Enter. 5) tail -f."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: using > when you meant >>, which silently wipes the file''s old contents. Mistake: getting stuck in less or nano - remember q quits less and Ctrl+X exits nano. Exit: (a) Which redirection operator appends to a file? (b) Which command shows only the last lines of a file? Answers: >> ; tail (for example tail -n 20 file)."}]}'::jsonb, '> overwrites a file from scratch; >> appends to the end. Reach for >> when you want to keep what is already there.', 13),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"From /home/noelia/projects, the relative path ../music points to...","options":["/home/noelia/projects/music","/home/noelia/music","/music","/home/music"],"answer":1,"explanation":".. climbs to /home/noelia first, then into music."},{"prompt":"Which single command creates the nested path a/b/c even when a and b do not exist yet?","options":["mkdir a/b/c","mkdir -p a/b/c","cp -r a/b/c","touch a/b/c"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The -p flag makes any missing parent directories along the path."},{"prompt":"To copy an entire directory named site to site_old, you run...","options":["cp site site_old","cp -r site site_old","mv site site_old","rm -r site site_old"],"answer":1,"explanation":"cp needs -r to copy a directory and its contents; without it cp refuses."},{"prompt":"After echo one > f.txt and then echo two >> f.txt, the file f.txt contains...","options":["only ''two''","only ''one''","''one'' then ''two''","nothing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"> writes ''one''; >> appends ''two'' on the next line, so both remain."},{"prompt":"Which command opens a long file in a scrollable pager you leave by pressing q?","options":["cat","less","echo","nano"],"answer":1,"explanation":"less pages through large files; press q to quit."},{"prompt":"In a path, the abbreviation ~ stands for...","options":["the root directory /","your home directory","the parent directory","the current directory"],"answer":1,"explanation":"~ expands to your home directory, e.g. /home/noelia."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'Permissions and ownership: chmod, chown, users and groups', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Read a permission string from ls -l, change permissions with chmod, and change ownership with chown. Warm-up: which command changes a file''s permission bits?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Every file has an owner, a group, and a permission string shown by ls -l, like -rwxr-xr--. The first character is the type (- for a file, d for a directory). The next nine are three groups of three: owner, group, others, each showing read (r), write (w), and execute (x). Those letters also have numbers: r=4, w=2, x=1, added per group. So rwx = 7, r-x = 5, r-- = 4, and rwxr-xr-- is 754. chmod sets permissions either numerically (chmod 755 file) or symbolically (chmod +x file to add execute). chown changes the owner, and chown user:group file changes both at once. Because these are protected actions, you often prefix them with sudo to run as the administrator."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Inspect a file: ls -l script.sh might show -rw-r--r-- 1 noelia noelia. Make it executable so you can run it: chmod +x script.sh, and it becomes -rwxr-xr-x. Set exact permissions numerically: chmod 644 notes.txt gives owner read+write (6) and group and others read (4). Give a script full owner rights and read+execute to the rest: chmod 755 deploy.sh. Change a file''s owner (needs admin): sudo chown noelia report.txt. Change owner and group together: sudo chown noelia:developers shared.txt. Check your groups: the groups command lists every group you belong to."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In rwxr-xr--, what may the group do? 2) What number equals rw-r--r--? 3) Which command makes a file executable without touching other bits? 4) What does chown noelia:staff file.txt change? 5) Why might chown need sudo? Answers: 1) Read and execute (r-x), but not write. 2) 644 - owner 6 (rw), group 4 (r), others 4 (r). 3) chmod +x file. 4) It sets the owner to noelia and the group to staff. 5) Changing ownership is an administrative action restricted to the superuser."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: using chmod 777 to ''fix'' a problem - that grants everyone full write access and is a security risk; prefer the least permission that works, like 644 or 755. Mistake: confusing chmod (permissions) with chown (ownership). Exit: (a) What numeric mode equals rwxr-xr-x? (b) Which command changes who owns a file? Answers: 755; chown."}]}'::jsonb, 'Read, write, and execute map to the numbers 4, 2, and 1. Add them for each of owner, group, and others - so 7 is read+write+execute and 5 is read+execute.', 14),
  (7, 'Pipes and filters: grep, sort, wc, cut', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Chain small commands with pipes to search, count, sort, and slice text without opening a single file. Warm-up: which single character joins two commands so the first feeds the second?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The Linux philosophy is many small tools that each do one job, joined with the pipe |. A filter reads text on its input and writes transformed text on its output. grep PATTERN prints only the lines that match a pattern; grep -i ignores case and grep -c counts matches. wc counts lines, words, and characters, and wc -l counts just lines. sort orders lines alphabetically, sort -n sorts numerically, and sort -r reverses. uniq collapses adjacent duplicate lines and pairs well with sort. cut -d DELIM -f N slices out a column from delimited text. Because these read standard input, you can pipe any command''s output straight into them."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Find matching lines in a file: grep error app.log. Count how many lines match, ignoring case: grep -ic error app.log. Count the lines in a file: wc -l data.csv. Build a pipeline: cat names.txt | sort | uniq lists each distinct name once, alphabetically. Pull the first column of a comma file: cut -d '','' -f 1 people.csv. Find the busiest values: sort access.log | uniq -c | sort -nr shows counts, most frequent first. Combine tools: grep ''404'' access.log | wc -l counts how many log lines contain 404."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which command prints only lines containing the word ''fail''? 2) How do you count just the number of lines in log.txt? 3) What does sort -n do differently from plain sort? 4) In cut -d '':'' -f 1 /etc/passwd, what does -d '':'' set? 5) Write a pipeline that lists the unique lines of a.txt in order. Answers: 1) grep fail (for example grep fail log.txt). 2) wc -l log.txt. 3) It sorts numerically, so 2 comes before 10 instead of after. 4) The field delimiter - columns are split on the colon. 5) sort a.txt | uniq."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: expecting uniq to remove all duplicates when they are scattered - it only collapses adjacent lines, so sort first. Mistake: forgetting -l on wc and getting three numbers instead of a line count. Exit: (a) Which command filters lines by a pattern? (b) What character pipes one command''s output into another? Answers: grep; the pipe |."}]}'::jsonb, 'A pipe connects the standard output of one command to the standard input of the next, so text flows left to right through each filter in turn.', 14),
  (8, 'Processes and system monitoring: ps, top, kill, systemctl', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"See what is running, identify a process by its PID, stop a stuck one, and check background services. Warm-up: which command shows a live, refreshing table of processes?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A process is a running program, identified by a PID. ps lists processes; ps aux shows every process on the system with its user, PID, and resource use. top (or the friendlier htop, if installed) shows a live table that refreshes, sorted by usage, so you can spot what is busy; press q to quit. kill PID asks a process to stop politely; if it ignores that, kill -9 PID forces it to stop immediately. A service (or daemon) is a long-running background process managed by systemd; systemctl controls services: systemctl status NAME reports whether one is running, and start, stop, restart, and enable manage it, usually with sudo."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"List all processes and find one: ps aux | grep firefox shows Firefox''s line, including its PID. Watch live usage: top, then press q to exit. Stop a process gently by its PID: kill 4820. If it will not stop, force it: kill -9 4820. Check a service: systemctl status ssh reports active (running) or inactive. Restart a web server after a config change: sudo systemctl restart nginx. Make a service start at every boot: sudo systemctl enable nginx. See what is listening and busy without leaving the terminal: top sorts the heaviest processes to the top by default."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What identifies a specific process to kill? 2) Which command lists every process with its user and PID? 3) What does kill -9 do that plain kill does not? 4) Which tool checks whether the ssh service is running? 5) How do you make nginx start automatically at boot? Answers: 1) Its PID (process ID). 2) ps aux. 3) It forces an immediate stop, giving the process no chance to ignore or clean up. 4) systemctl status ssh. 5) sudo systemctl enable nginx."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reaching for kill -9 first - try a plain kill so the program can shut down cleanly, and force only if it hangs. Mistake: confusing stop (ends the service now) with disable (stops it starting at boot). Exit: (a) What number does most process commands use as their target? (b) Which command reports whether a systemd service is active? Answers: the PID; systemctl status NAME."}]}'::jsonb, 'Every running process has a numeric PID (process ID). Most commands that act on a process, such as kill, take that PID as their target.', 13),
  (9, 'Checkpoint 2', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The permission mode rwxr-xr-x written as a number is...","options":["644","755","777","700"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Owner rwx=7, group r-x=5, others r-x=5, giving 755."},{"prompt":"Which command adds execute permission to a script without changing its other bits?","options":["chmod 000 s.sh","chmod +x s.sh","chown +x s.sh","chmod -x s.sh"],"answer":1,"explanation":"chmod +x adds the execute bit; chmod -x would remove it."},{"prompt":"The pipeline sort a.txt | uniq is needed instead of uniq alone because uniq...","options":["cannot read files","only removes duplicate lines that are adjacent","sorts numerically","counts words"],"answer":1,"explanation":"uniq collapses only neighbouring duplicates, so you sort first to bring duplicates together."},{"prompt":"To count how many lines in log.txt contain the word error, you run...","options":["grep -c error log.txt","wc -l error log.txt","sort error log.txt","cut error log.txt"],"answer":0,"explanation":"grep -c counts matching lines; grep error log.txt | wc -l works too."},{"prompt":"A stuck process refuses a plain kill. The command that forces it to stop is...","options":["kill -0 PID","kill -9 PID","stop PID","systemctl PID"],"answer":1,"explanation":"kill -9 sends the non-ignorable signal that forces immediate termination."},{"prompt":"Which command reports whether the nginx service is currently running?","options":["ps nginx","systemctl status nginx","top nginx","grep nginx"],"answer":1,"explanation":"systemctl status NAME shows active (running) or inactive for a systemd service."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (10, 'Package management and installing software', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Update the package list, search for software, and install, remove, and update packages on a Debian-family system. Warm-up: on Ubuntu, which tool installs a package?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A package manager installs software and every library it depends on from trusted online repositories, so you rarely download programs by hand. On Debian and Ubuntu the tool is apt; on Fedora and Red Hat it is dnf; the ideas match even when the names differ. First refresh the local catalogue with apt update so you know what versions exist. Then apt install NAME adds a package and its dependencies, apt remove NAME takes it away, and apt upgrade updates everything installed. apt search WORD finds packages by keyword. Because these change system files, you prefix them with sudo. Some distributions also offer newer formats like snap or flatpak that bundle an app with its dependencies."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Refresh the catalogue first: sudo apt update. Install a tool: sudo apt install htop pulls in htop and anything it needs. Search before installing: apt search json shows matching packages. Remove something: sudo apt remove htop. Update every installed package: sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade runs the refresh, then the upgrade, only if the first succeeds. Check what you have: apt list --installed | grep python lists installed Python-related packages. On a Fedora machine the same install would read sudo dnf install htop."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which command refreshes the package catalogue on Ubuntu? 2) How do you install the package named git? 3) Why do these commands usually start with sudo? 4) What does apt search do? 5) On Fedora, which tool replaces apt? Answers: 1) sudo apt update. 2) sudo apt install git. 3) Installing or removing software changes the whole system, which requires administrator rights. 4) It searches available packages by keyword. 5) dnf (for example sudo dnf install git)."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: running apt install without an apt update first, so you may fetch stale version information. Mistake: forgetting sudo and getting a permission-denied error. Exit: (a) Which command installs a package on Ubuntu? (b) Why is sudo needed for it? Answers: sudo apt install NAME; because installing software modifies the whole system and needs administrator rights."}]}'::jsonb, 'Installing or removing system software changes the whole machine, so these commands almost always need sudo (administrator rights).', 12),
  (11, 'Shell scripting basics: variables, conditionals, and loops', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Write a runnable bash script that stores values in variables, makes decisions with if, and repeats work with a loop. Warm-up: which chmod command makes a script file executable?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A shell script is a text file of commands. The first line, called the shebang, is #!/bin/bash and tells the system which interpreter to use. You make the file runnable with chmod +x script.sh and start it with ./script.sh. A variable is set with name=value (no spaces) and read back as $name; \"$name\" in double quotes keeps spaces safe. Scripts read arguments as $1, $2, and so on. A conditional runs code only when a test passes: if [ \"$x\" -gt 10 ]; then ... fi, where -gt means greater-than, -eq equal, and -lt less-than for numbers. A for loop repeats over a list: for f in *.txt; do ... done walks each matching file. A while loop repeats as long as a test holds."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A first script, saved as greet.sh: line one #!/bin/bash, line two name=\"Noelia\", line three echo \"Hello, $name\". Make it run: chmod +x greet.sh then ./greet.sh prints Hello, Noelia. Use an argument: a script with echo \"You passed: $1\" run as ./show.sh hello prints You passed: hello. A conditional: if [ \"$1\" -gt 100 ]; then echo big; else echo small; fi. A for loop over files: for f in *.log; do echo \"Found $f\"; done prints a line per log file. A counting while loop: i=1; while [ \"$i\" -le 3 ]; do echo \"$i\"; i=$((i+1)); done prints 1, 2, 3, where $((...)) does arithmetic."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What is the shebang line for a bash script? 2) Write the line that stores the word blue in a variable called color. 3) How do you print that variable''s value? 4) Which test operator means ''greater than'' for numbers? 5) What does for f in *.txt; do echo $f; done do? Answers: 1) #!/bin/bash. 2) color=blue (no spaces around =). 3) echo $color (or the safer echo \"$color\"). 4) -gt. 5) It prints the name of every file ending in .txt in the current directory, one per line."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: writing name = value with spaces around the equals sign, which bash reads as a command instead of an assignment. Mistake: forgetting chmod +x, so ./script.sh reports permission denied. Exit: (a) How do you read the value of a variable named path? (b) What must you do to a script file before ./ will run it? Answers: with $path (or \"$path\"); make it executable with chmod +x."}]}'::jsonb, 'Set a variable with no spaces around the equals sign (name=value), but read it back with a dollar sign ($name). name = value with spaces is an error.', 15),
  (12, 'Environment, PATH, and SSH basics', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Read and set environment variables, understand how PATH finds commands, and connect to a remote machine with ssh. Warm-up: when you type ls, how does the shell know where the ls program lives?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Environment variables are named values the shell and programs read; you see them all with env and one with echo $NAME. The most important is PATH, a colon-separated list of directories the shell searches to find a command; which ls shows the exact program chosen. You set a variable for the current shell with export NAME=value, and to make it stick across sessions you add that line to ~/.bashrc, your shell''s start-up file. To reach another computer, ssh user@host opens a secure remote shell; the first connection asks you to confirm the host''s fingerprint. Instead of typing a password every time, you can create a key pair with ssh-keygen and copy the public half to the server with ssh-copy-id user@host, after which login is automatic and safer."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Show your PATH: echo $PATH prints something like /usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin. Find which program runs for a name: which python3 might print /usr/bin/python3. Set a variable for this session: export EDITOR=nano. Make it permanent: add export EDITOR=nano to the end of ~/.bashrc, then reload with source ~/.bashrc. Add a folder of your own scripts to PATH: export PATH=\"$HOME/bin:$PATH\" puts $HOME/bin first. Connect to a server: ssh noelia@example.com opens a shell there; type exit to return. Set up key login: ssh-keygen creates the pair, and ssh-copy-id noelia@example.com installs your public key."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which command prints the value of PATH? 2) What separates the directories inside PATH? 3) How do you set an environment variable for the current shell only? 4) Which file makes a variable persist across new terminals? 5) What does ssh noelia@server do? Answers: 1) echo $PATH. 2) The colon character (:). 3) export NAME=value. 4) ~/.bashrc (reloaded with source ~/.bashrc or a new terminal). 5) It opens a secure remote shell on server as the user noelia."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: overwriting PATH with export PATH=/my/dir instead of appending, which hides the system commands; always include :$PATH. Mistake: expecting an export to survive a new terminal without adding it to ~/.bashrc. Exit: (a) What does PATH hold? (b) Which command opens a secure shell on a remote host? Answers: a colon-separated list of directories the shell searches for commands; ssh user@host."}]}'::jsonb, 'PATH is a list of directories separated by colons. When you type a command, the shell searches those directories in order and runs the first match it finds.', 13),
  (13, 'Checkpoint 3', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Before installing new software on Ubuntu, it is wise to first run...","options":["sudo apt remove","sudo apt update","apt search","chmod +x"],"answer":1,"explanation":"sudo apt update refreshes the catalogue so you install current versions."},{"prompt":"In a bash script, the correct way to store the number 5 in a variable n is...","options":["n = 5","n=5","$n=5","set n 5"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Assignment uses no spaces around =; you read it back as $n."},{"prompt":"The very first line of a bash script should be...","options":["#include <bash>","#!/bin/bash","run bash","// bash"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The shebang #!/bin/bash tells the system which interpreter to use."},{"prompt":"PATH is best described as...","options":["a single program","a colon-separated list of directories the shell searches for commands","your home directory","a permission bit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The shell scans PATH''s directories in order to locate the command you typed."},{"prompt":"The loop for f in *.txt; do echo $f; done will...","options":["delete every .txt file","print the name of each .txt file","count words in one file","open an editor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The for loop iterates the matching filenames and echoes each one."},{"prompt":"To open a secure shell on a remote host as user noelia, you run...","options":["ssh noelia@host","scp noelia host","ping noelia@host","telnet host"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ssh user@host starts an encrypted remote session."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (14, 'Capstone: build a small useful script', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll build","text":"A backup script called backup.sh that takes a directory name as its argument, copies it into a timestamped folder under your home directory, and prints a short report of how many files it saved. It ties together everything so far: variables, arguments, a conditional, a command substitution, and file commands."},{"heading":"The pieces you already know","text":"Shebang and permissions: start with #!/bin/bash and later run chmod +x backup.sh. Argument: the folder to back up arrives as $1. A guard clause: if the caller forgot the argument or the folder is missing, print a usage message and stop. A timestamp: date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S prints something like 20260720_143000, and command substitution $(...) captures it into a variable. Copying: cp -r copies the whole tree. Counting: piping a file list into wc -l reports how many items were handled."},{"heading":"A model script to study, then rebuild yourself","text":"Line by line: #!/bin/bash ; then a guard: if [ -z \"$1\" ]; then echo \"Usage: ./backup.sh <folder>\"; exit 1; fi (here -z tests for an empty argument). Next a second guard: if [ ! -d \"$1\" ]; then echo \"No such folder: $1\"; exit 1; fi (-d tests that the path is a directory). Then build the destination: stamp=$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S) and dest=\"$HOME/backup_${stamp}\". Do the work: mkdir -p \"$dest\" and cp -r \"$1\" \"$dest\". Finally report: count=$(ls \"$dest/$1\" | wc -l) and echo \"Backed up $count items from $1 into $dest\". Save it, run chmod +x backup.sh, and test with ./backup.sh notes."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Rebuild the script from scratch without copying, then extend it. 1) Why does -z \"$1\" guard against a missing argument? 2) What does $(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S) put into stamp? 3) Why wrap \"$1\" and \"$dest\" in double quotes? 4) Add a line that also writes the report into a log file with >> . 5) How would you make cp show each file as it copies? Answers: 1) -z is true when the string is empty, so it catches the case where no folder was passed. 2) A timestamp string like 20260720_143000, making each backup folder unique. 3) Quotes keep names with spaces from being split into several arguments. 4) For example echo \"Backed up $count items\" >> \"$HOME/backup.log\" to append a record. 5) Add the -v (verbose) flag: cp -rv \"$1\" \"$dest\"."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: skipping the guard clauses, so the script fails confusingly when run with no argument or a bad path. Mistake: leaving variables unquoted, which breaks on folders whose names contain spaces. Exit: (a) Which test checks that $1 is an existing directory? (b) What does command substitution $(...) do? Answers: [ -d \"$1\" ]; it runs the command inside and substitutes its output, letting you capture a value like the timestamp into a variable."}]}'::jsonb, 'Build the script one line at a time and run it after each addition. A script that works in small pieces is far easier to fix than one written all at once.', 16),
  (15, 'Final quiz: driving Linux from the terminal', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Reading /var/log/syslog left to right, the file named syslog lives inside...","options":["the home directory","the log directory under var, under root","the etc directory","a relative path from your current folder"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Start at root /, into var, into log, at the file syslog - an absolute path."},{"prompt":"Which command both moves and renames files depending on the destination?","options":["cp","mv","rm","ls"],"answer":1,"explanation":"mv moves a file, and moving it to a new name in place renames it."},{"prompt":"To append a command''s output to an existing file without erasing it, use...","options":[">",">>","<","|"],"answer":1,"explanation":">> appends; > would overwrite the file from scratch."},{"prompt":"The permission string rw-r--r-- as a numeric mode is...","options":["644","755","777","600"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Owner rw=6, group r=4, others r=4, giving 644."},{"prompt":"The pipeline grep ''404'' access.log | wc -l produces...","options":["the first 404 line","the count of lines containing 404","the file sorted","the newest log entry"],"answer":1,"explanation":"grep selects lines with 404 and wc -l counts them."},{"prompt":"A process is misbehaving and ignores a normal kill. You force it to stop with...","options":["kill -1 PID","kill -9 PID","kill -0 PID","kill PID PID"],"answer":1,"explanation":"kill -9 sends the signal a process cannot ignore, ending it immediately."},{"prompt":"On an Ubuntu system, installing the package curl is done with...","options":["sudo apt install curl","apt search curl","curl install","chmod +x curl"],"answer":0,"explanation":"sudo apt install NAME installs the package and its dependencies."},{"prompt":"In bash, the value of a variable named city is read as...","options":["city","$city","&city","city()"],"answer":1,"explanation":"You set it as city=value and read it back with a dollar sign, $city."},{"prompt":"The shell finds the program for a typed command by searching the directories listed in...","options":["HOME","PATH","PWD","USER"],"answer":1,"explanation":"PATH is the colon-separated list of directories the shell searches in order."},{"prompt":"To connect to a remote server called example.com as user noelia over an encrypted link, you run...","options":["ssh noelia@example.com","cp noelia example.com","ping example.com","sudo example.com"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ssh user@host opens a secure remote shell on that machine."}]}'::jsonb, null, 12)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0002-0000-4000-8000-000000000018' and l.position = v.pos
